{#title}
^1 The Markit Language
^3 A Guide by Example

This document demonstrates every feature of the Markit markup language. It is itself a valid Markit document, and can be compiled to HTML or plain text.

{#1}
Markit is a markup language for preserving early texts. It is designed for scholars working with historical source material: manuscripts, first editions, and critical texts. It handles the things those texts need {--} foreign languages, editorial annotations, page breaks, footnotes {--} while staying readable as plain text.

{#subtitle}
^2 How to Read This Guide

{#2}
Each section below covers a feature of the language. The text you are reading is the Markit source; compile it to see the rendered output.

## Structure

[metadata]
note = "Every text in the hierarchy can carry its own metadata."

{#title}
^1 Document Structure

{#1}
A Markit document is a tree of _texts_. The top-level text starts with a single \#, its children with \#\#, their children with \#\#\#, and so on. Each text has a unique ID written after the \# characters. This document's top-level ID is "MarkitGuide" and you are reading a child text with the ID "MarkitGuide.Structure". Parent text IDs are prepended to child IDs to ensure uniqueness across the whole document.

{#subtitle}
^2 Content Blocks

{#2}
Within each text, content is organised into _blocks_. A block begins with a block tag: a \# character and an ID wrapped in curly braces, like \{#1\} or \{#title\}. There are four types of block, determined by the ID:

{#3}
*Title blocks* (\{#title\}) contain the title of the text. There can be at most one per text, and it must be the first block. Title blocks can contain both headings and regular paragraph content.

{#4}
*Subtitle blocks* (\{#subtitle\}) mark section headings within the text. You can have as many as you like; the compiler numbers them automatically (subtitle1, subtitle2, etc.) to keep IDs unique.

{#5}
*Paragraph blocks* have any other ID (conventionally numbered: \{#1\}, \{#2\}, etc.). These hold the main body text.

{#6}
*Footnote blocks* have IDs beginning with "n" (e.g. \{#n1\}, \{#n2\}). They must appear after all other blocks in the text. See the Footnotes section below.

## Metadata

{#title}
^1 Metadata

{#1}
A text can optionally include a metadata block immediately after its ID line. The metadata block begins with the header \[metadata\] and contains key-value pairs using a subset of TOML syntax.

{#subtitle}
^2 Supported Value Types

{#2}
Booleans: reviewed = true or reviewed = false. //
Numbers: volume = 3 or firstPublished = 1721. //
Strings: title = \"An Essay Concerning Human Understanding\". //
Arrays: tags = \[\"philosophy\", \"epistemology\"\].

{#3}
Arrays can span multiple lines. All elements in an array must be the same type {--} you cannot mix strings and numbers in a single array.

{#subtitle}
^2 Nested Metadata

{#4}
Metadata supports one level of nesting using \[metadata.key\] headers. This document's root text uses nested metadata to store links separately from top-level fields.

{#subtitle}
^2 Block Metadata

{#5, scope="block", reviewed=true}
Individual blocks can also carry metadata, written as a comma-separated list of key=value pairs inside the block tag after the ID. The same value types are supported as for text metadata: booleans, numbers, strings, and homogeneous arrays. This block's own tag is an example.

## Headings

{#title}
^1 Headings
^4 Title and subtitle blocks can contain heading lines

{#1}
A heading line starts with a caret and a number from 1 to 6, indicating the size (1 is the largest, 6 the smallest). Consecutive heading lines form a single heading group. Look at the source of this block's title to see how a multi-line heading works.

{#subtitle}
^2 Subtitle Headings Work the Same Way
^5 You can mix sizes within a heading group

{#2}
Headings are only permitted inside title and subtitle blocks. If you try to use a heading line (^1, ^2, etc.) inside a paragraph block, it will raise an error.

## Formatting

{#title}
^1 Inline Formatting

{#1}
Markit provides several inline formatting markers. They can be *nested*: for example, _an italic phrase with *a bold word* inside it_.

{#subtitle}
^2 Bold and Italic

{#2}
Surround text with asterisks for *bold* and underscores for _italic_. Unlike Markdown, the distinction is based on the character, not how many of them you use. Bold formatting is intended to be used to represent small capitals in the source text.

{#subtitle}
^2 Quotations

{#3}
Inline quotations use double quotes: Locke writes that "the mind is furnished with ideas" through experience alone.

{#subtitle}
^2 Citations

{#4}
Citations are enclosed in square brackets. A reference like [Essay II.i.2] renders as a citation element, semantically distinct from plain text.

{#subtitle}
^2 Editorial Markup

{#5}
Scholars annotating texts can mark ++editorial insertions++ with double plus signs, --editorial deletions-- with double hyphens, ==highlighted passages== with double equals signs, and ??uncertain readings?? with double question marks. Three question marks in a row (???) mark illegible text.

{#subtitle}
^2 Margin Comments

{#6}
Text enclosed in \@ symbols represents a margin comment or aside: @This note appeared in the margin of the original manuscript.@

## BlockQuotes

{#title}
^1 Block Quotations

{#1}
A line beginning with > is a block quotation. Consecutive > lines form a single quotation:

> All our ideas are either simple or complex. The simple ones enter
> the mind through sensation and reflection.

{#2}
A blank > line separates paragraphs within a block quotation:

> The first paragraph of the quotation.
>
> The second paragraph, still part of the same block quote.

{#3}
Inline formatting works inside block quotations: bold, italic, citations, and all other markers function normally.

## Whitespace

{#title}
^1 Whitespace and Line Breaks

{#1}
Markit collapses whitespace. Line breaks in the source are treated as spaces, and multiple consecutive spaces become a single space. This means you can wrap long lines freely without affecting the output.

{#subtitle}
^2 Explicit Line Breaks

{#2}
Use // to force a line break. This is essential for verse: //
//
Tell me, O muse, of that ingenious hero who travelled far and wide //
after he had sacked the famous town of Troy.

{#subtitle}
^2 Spaces

{#3}
A single tilde (~) produces a non-breaking space. Two consecutive tildes (~~) produce an em space, useful for indentation.

## ForeignText

{#title}
^1 Foreign Text

{#1}
Source texts frequently include words and passages in other languages. Generic foreign text is enclosed in dollar signs: $cogito ergo sum$.

{#subtitle}
^2 Language Codes

{#2}
Language wrappers mark text semantically. Use a language code prefix to tag the language:

{#3}
*Greek* ($grc:philosophia$) marks text as Ancient Greek. *Latin* ($la:Roma$) and *French* ($fr:raison$) work the same way. Any ISO 639 language code can be used.

{#subtitle}
^2 Character Mode

{#4}
Curly braces activate character mode, which provides shorthand for characters that are awkward to type. Diacritic markers follow the base character: acute {e/}, grave {a`}, circumflex {a^}, diaeresis {a"}. Digraphs produce ligatures: {ae}, {AE}, {oe}, {OE}, {c,}, {C,}. Special symbols: {$} (section mark), {-} (en dash), {--} (em dash).

{#5}
Character mode spans as many characters as you like: {a/e/i/o/u/} renders all five vowels with acute accents, and {aesthetics} applies the ae ligature automatically.

{#subtitle}
^2 Greek Mode

{#6}
Double curly braces activate Greek mode, which applies Latin-to-Greek transliteration in addition to character mode processing: {{philosophia}}, {{logos}}, {{lo/gos}}. Greek-specific diacritics are supported: smooth breathing {{a)}}, rough breathing {{a(}}, iota subscript {{a|}}, circumflex {{a^}}. Full example: {{a)nthrwpos}}, {{a(rmoni/a}}.

{#subtitle}
^2 Formatting Inside Foreign Text

{#7}
Inline formatting works inside foreign text: $grc:*philosophia*$ renders as bold text tagged as Greek. Line breaks (//) also work within language wrappers.

## SpecialCharacters

{#title}
^1 Page Breaks

{#1}
The || marker indicates a page break from the original copytext. In HTML output, it renders as an empty span you can style with CSS. In plain text, it is removed. Here is a page break in action: ||some text continued on the next page.

{#2}
A page break with a folio reference uses |ref| syntax: |12r| marks the start of folio 12 recto.

## Footnotes

{#title}
^1 Footnotes

{#1}
Footnotes are referenced in the body text with angle brackets: Locke explains this concept<n1> in a later chapter, and revisits it again<n2> in Book IV.

{#2}
Multiple references to the same footnote are allowed: this sentence<n1> also cites footnote 1.

{#n1}
[Essay II.xxvii.9]. See also the discussion in Nidditch's critical edition, pp. 328{-}348.

{#n2}
[Essay IV.ix.3]. Locke's argument here relies on the distinction established earlier.

## Escaping

{#title}
^1 Escaping Special Characters

{#1}
Prefix any special character with a backslash to include it literally. For example: \* for a literal asterisk, \_ for a literal underscore, \" for a literal double quote, \[ and \] for literal square brackets, \{ and \} for literal curly braces, and \\ for a literal backslash.

{#2}
This is necessary when your source text contains characters that Markit would otherwise interpret as markup. For instance, to write about the expression 5 \* 3 = 15 without triggering bold formatting, you escape the asterisks.
